Cookie paths determine which URLs on your backend are allowed to receive a particular cookie.

In simple terms, cookie paths control where the browser sends cookies.

1. ACCESS_COOKIE_PATH=/
   ACCESS_COOKIE_PATH=/

/ means the cookie is available to all paths on your backend.

For example:
API endpoint Access cookie sent?
/api/products Yes
/api/users Yes
/api/orders Yes
/api/auth/profile Yes

This is useful because the access token is required for accessing protected APIs throughout your application.

2. REFRESH_COOKIE_PATH=/api/auth
   REFRESH_COOKIE_PATH=/api/auth

This means the refresh token is sent only to URLs under /api/auth.

For example:
API endpoint Refresh cookie sent?
/api/auth/refresh Yes
/api/auth/logout Yes
/api/auth/login Yes
/api/products No
/api/users No

This restricts unnecessary exposure of the refresh token to other API endpoints.

# Cookie settings

COOKIE_HTTP_ONLY=true
COOKIE_SECURE=false
COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax
COOKIE_DOMAIN=

Setting Purpose
COOKIE_HTTP_ONLY=true
Prevents JavaScript from accessing cookies. Protects against cookie theft through XSS.

COOKIE_SECURE=false
Allows cookies over HTTP. Use false for localhost development and true for HTTPS production.

COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax
Restricts cookies in most cross-site requests, helping protect against CSRF attacks.

COOKIE_DOMAIN=  
Specifies which domain can receive the cookie. Empty means the browser uses the host that sets the cookie.

Recommended configuration
Local development:
COOKIE_HTTP_ONLY=true
COOKIE_SECURE=false
COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax
COOKIE_DOMAIN=

Production (HTTPS):
COOKIE_HTTP_ONLY=true
COOKIE_SECURE=true
COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax
COOKIE_DOMAIN=yourdomain.com

Remember:

- HTTPOnly = JavaScript protection.
- Secure = HTTPS only.
- SameSite = Cross-site cookie protection.
- Domain = Which domain receives the cookie.

# COOKIE_SAME_SITE

The COOKIE_SAME_SITE setting controls when browsers send cookies in cross-site requests.
Value Meaning
strict Cookies are sent only in same-site requests. Stronger cross-site protection.
lax Cookies are sent in same-site requests and certain cross-site top-level navigations.
none Cookies are allowed in cross-site requests. Requires COOKIE_SECURE=true (HTTPS).

My recommendation:
COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax

Lax is a good default for most applications because it balances security and usability.

# React frontend

CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

Meaning: Specifies which frontend origin is allowed to communicate with your Express backend.

- http://localhost:5173 – Your React + Vite frontend URL.
- Only requests from this configured origin are allowed by your CORS policy.
  Example:

React frontend: localhost:5173

Express backend: localhost:5000

CORS allows your React application to access backend APIs running on port 5000.

Production: Replace this with your actual frontend domain.

# satisfies

const signAccess = (user: SafeUser): string =>
jwt.sign(
{
userId: user.id,
email: user.email,
type: "access",
} satisfies AccessPayload,
ACCESS_SECRET,
{
expiresIn: ACCESS_EXPIRES as SignOptions["expiresIn"],
}
);
In TypeScript, satisfies checks whether an object matches a specified type
without changing the object's inferred type.

What does it do?

1. Creates an object containing userId, email, and type.
2. Checks whether the object satisfies the AccessPayload interface.
3. Reports a TypeScript compilation error if the object doesn't match the required structure.

---

JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN=10s
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN=30s

ACCESS_COOKIE_MAX_AGE=10000
REFRESH_COOKIE_MAX_AGE=30000

Make sure your .env also has:
REFRESH_COOKIE_MAX_AGE=30000
ACCESS_COOKIE_MAX_AGE=10000

Otherwise, the browser may remove the refresh cookie before its JWT expires.
